 we'll give out your details at the end of the show.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:37.240
 But would you please just give a round-up

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:39.400
 of your academic professional background

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:43.680
 so people can get an idea of where your work is coming from?

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.360
 I was studying and teaching in the humanities

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:51.800
 for about 10 years before I decided

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:55.880
 to go to graduate school in biology, which

00:07:55.880 --> 00:08:02.800
 was in 1968 to 1972, University of Oregon,

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:08.440
 where I got a PhD in reproductive physiology

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:10.520
 and aging.

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:14.720
 And my intention had been to study

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:18.840
 nerve biology when I went to the university,

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:24.280
 following up on ideas of how language and brain work.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:30.640
 But because of dogmatism in the nerve biology section,

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.520
 I ended up with reproductive physiology

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:40.400
 as a way to concentrate on interactions of hormones,

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:50.880
 metabolism, and development. And over the next almost 50 years,

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:56.760
 I've been continuing to study those areas.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:01.280
 And you've had quite a long history

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:03.840
 of actually consulting with people.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:07.760
 And firsthand, I won't say experimental,

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:11.280
 because it's not experimental, but first-hand guidance

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:16.560
 with philosophies that you have formulated over time here.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:22.640
 And much of what I've understood from you in the last 10 years

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:25.400
 has been related to energy production.

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:27.120
 I think I would sum that up.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:32.720
 In the terms of creative energy production and stemming

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.200
 the dissolution of energy, whether that is from a dietary

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:40.480
 perspective or a supplemental perspective.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:45.240
 But tonight's show, again, it piqued my interest.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:49.360
 And I cannot claim to be a scientist or knowledgeable

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:51.120
 in any way on the subject.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.720
 But I have some definite questions I'd like to ask you

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:58.920
 and to also help to understand the subject a little more

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:02.360
 simply for listeners who perhaps don't have too much

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:03.400
 of a science background.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:07.760
 But a lot of what you can bring out in epigenetics

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:10.760
 is very relevant in terms of understanding

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:12.080
 the simple concepts of it.

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:17.360
 So going to the term "epigenetics,"

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:19.640
 it's basically-- I think I could sum it up by saying

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:25.520
 it's a heritable phenotype, the change of which does not

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:27.920
 involve alterations in the DNA sequence.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:32.680
 Now, before I let you take the floor with the concept

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:34.560
 of epigenetics, and I start to question you,

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.560
 this one people to understand the two differences

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.440
 of phenotype and genotype.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:43.000
 When we're talking about genes and DNA

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.280
 and what they code for and what they do,

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:48.160
 the genotype gives the instruction.

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:53.160
 And the phenotype in an organism is its appearance.

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:59.920
 So I saw that an account-- and the word "environmental

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:03.320
 enrichment," which I know you'll bring out in the context

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:13.360
 of altering or rather adapting to the changes in epigenetics.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:17.920
 The epigenetics, themselves, may account

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.360
 for a significant fraction of what

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:24.120
 they term the missing heritability as DNA mutations

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:28.120
 only explained a minority of the expected heritable fraction.

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:29.680
 And they said that this discrepancy

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.040
 is known as the missing heritability.

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:39.080
 So when you describe epigenetics for our listeners, some of which

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:42.600
 may not be as science-based, if you

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:47.920
 could describe the basic outlines of epigenetics

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:52.240
 and how, whether it's DNA changes in methylation

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:56.240
 or the histones that the genes wrap around

00:11:56.240 --> 00:12:00.680
 or this non-coding RNA that get altered

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:06.880
 and how the changes to those bring about profound changes

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:10.080
 in genetic information and what gets turned on

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:16.600
 and what gets turned off and how this epigenetic change really

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:19.480
 alters the structure of our consciousness, I think,

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:23.040
 in a lot of ways, or conversely, our consciousness

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:25.680
 can alter the structure of our epigenetics.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:28.240
 So would you just describe what it

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:31.080
 is that you understand as epigenetics

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:36.400
 and how they play into our health?

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:42.720
 The established powers in biology

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:47.440
 have been very resistant to the idea

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:54.800
 that there could be any transgenerational influence

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:56.600
 of experience.

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:04.680
 And that has involved even reducing and emptying

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:09.720
 the idea of what happens during adaptation.

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:14.520
 Why does exercise build stronger muscles

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:22.800
 and what happens during learning and developing new ideas?

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:31.320
 The very idea of change was resisted powerfully

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:39.080
 by the people who saw the organism as a compilation

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:44.840
 of specific traits once developed in infancy

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:48.640
 or achieving maturity.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:52.720
 The idea was that our traits and our parts

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:55.600
 were like a clock mechanism.

00:13:55.600 --> 00:14:00.680
 The parts operated, allowed the machine to work.

00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:05.200
 But nothing very deep was changing all

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:09.560
 during the adult lifetime.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:16.760
 And that has an extensive ideological background

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:26.240
 in which the organism was seen as nothing

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:31.680
 but a compilation of parts, each of which

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:35.680
 is eternal and unchangeable.

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:41.720
 And the idea that the organism might be in some way

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:48.480
 deeply changing after it has achieved maturity

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:55.240
 puts in doubt the whole idea that the immortal genes

00:14:55.240 --> 00:15:00.000
 specify the traits that together make up

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.000
 the machine of the organism.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:05.560
 The idea of the machine is intended

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:13.280
 to eliminate the idea that the parts are constantly changing.

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:20.400
 But in reality, when you look at the experience of yourself

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:24.200
 or of other organisms, you see that every day there

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:27.440
 is learning and change.

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:32.480
 So you can't really do anything twice

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:36.920
 because the second time you do it is you're a different person.

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:47.320
 And this idea really has a great biological significance

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:50.880
 that every moment of your experience,

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:56.280
 you're in development all the way through your lifespan.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:16:00.840
 And the immune system is constantly

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:06.360
 operating and recording your interactions

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:12.400
 with antigenic material or possible allergens

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:17.960
 or immunological stimulants the same way

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:23.520
 axons and experiences change the way you interpret the world.

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:27.920
 The structure of your brain is constantly changing.

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:31.800
 And that interacts with your constantly changing immune

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.640
 system.

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:38.720
 And all the rest of your body is moment to moment

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:41.280
 undergoing adaptation.

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:50.680
 And it isn't just a technological thing.

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:57.800
 The ideas that are constantly changing in your mind

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:01.840
 are actually changing the properties of the brain

00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:10.480
 cells, your immune cells, and probably all of the cells.

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:13.480
 No one has looked at every cell in the body,

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:17.560
 but pretty much every cell in the body,

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:26.880
 every functional cell is likely to have a fine, nervous fiber.

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:32.280
 So the body isn't just chemically

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:37.920
 communicating like a swarm of insects

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:41.160
 attracted to the smell of honey or something,

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:47.760
 but there is a very deep penetration of fine nerve

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:51.480
 fibers into all of the tissues, integrating

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:58.800
 the whole body and its chemistry with the conscious purpose

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:03.960
 and ongoing behavior of the organism.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:09.040
 So any little event happening in your body

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:15.920
 is also adding to the sum of development.

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:26.680
 Technically, people are using epigenetics

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:33.120
 just to mean certain chemical changes that persist

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:39.800
 from the state of one cell after that cell divides.

00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:47.320
 But the persistence of a physiological state

00:18:47.320 --> 00:18:53.880
 from moment to moment is really the simplest and most basic idea

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:56.000
 of epigenetics.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:02.960
 Every moment we inherit the whole past of our organism

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:09.280
 and the fact that we don't change drastically

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:15.640
 and unrecognizably is that there is a logic and a momentum

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:23.360
 in these developmental changes that the whole system is

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:33.680
 working in integration and with a rational set of goals.

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:39.000
 The extreme way and analogy biologically

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:44.520
 would be the transformation of a tadpole to a frog.

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:53.040
 That involves epigenetic changes of a very drastic sort.

00:19:53.800 --> 00:20:02.600
 But it's good to keep that in mind as hidden outside

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:05.400
 our immune system and nervous system

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:10.360
 are having these constant little transformations

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:13.680
 or metamorphoses.

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:17.760
 So it's not as drastic as a tadpole turning into a frog.

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:23.000
 But our immune system and brain in particular

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:28.680
 are recognizably different over very short time spans.

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:34.320
 And what you said a few moments ago caught my attention.

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:38.720
 And I kind of tried to sum it up as you--

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:43.320
 and again, this is not preaching or anything

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:46.120
 to those who have never really thought about the subject.

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:47.960
 But I think it's a very good point

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:52.120
 in explaining adaptation and epigenetics

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:55.240
 and how it affects us psychologically

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:59.120
 in terms of what we believe.

00:20:59.120 --> 00:21:03.640
 It reminds me that you are what you believe yourself to be

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:10.120
 and that the subject of positive reinforcement or affirmations,

00:21:10.120 --> 00:21:14.360
 positive affirmations, are seemingly a roadmap

00:21:14.360 --> 00:21:17.320
 to a better future.

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:20.720
 Because that is a way, in many ways,

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:26.240
 I think, of changing your perception, your outlook,

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:28.880
 your mental state, and your physical state

00:21:28.880 --> 00:21:34.560
 therein by epigenetic changes that can occur purely

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.960
 through positive reinforcement.

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:40.920
 Positive or negative.

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:43.360
 The environment, to a great extent,

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:49.600
 is imposing unpleasant degenerative dangers on us.

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:56.640
 And for example, if you put an animal in isolation,

00:21:56.640 --> 00:22:03.040
 social distancing, the animal becomes more inflamed.

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:05.880
 The whole immune system is deranged

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:08.840
 in the direction of inflammation just

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:17.320
 by the depressing experience of being isolated from misfriends.

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:24.320
 And if you reverse that, do the opposite,

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:29.040
 and give them lots of fun social contacts and lots

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:34.840
 of varied things to do, an enriched environment,

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:41.000
 that suppresses, that ends the inflammatory processes

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:46.960
 and starts the organism back on the constructive developmental

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:49.520
 process away from the degenerative processes.

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:54.840
 So in terms of going--

00:22:54.840 --> 00:23:02.040
 These ideas have been known to common people

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:03.040
 for hundreds of years.

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:14.520
 Lamarck, for example, said that if an organism needs

00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:18.840
 longer legs or stronger arms or whatever,

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:21.520
 it will develop them by striving.

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:27.760
 Basically, exercise improves the physiology and structure.

